we were very fascinated have been for a  while  with the sort of social thrillers of the  70s the coolest trilogy of Clue all the  President's Men in the parallax view and  I think we live in a time that's really  ripe for taking that sort of social  anxiety and putting it through the mill  in the summer of 2009 not as research  just but just us living our lives we  were we had been out in LA and it's all  had just gotten out of the American Film  Institute wanted to direct and I wanted  to act but those aren't exactly jobs you  can just go out and get so we were  jobless and we were young and we were  reading a lot about anarchists  manifestos online and about the sort of  freaking world and we were intrigued and  so we we set off to have an adventure  and we packed backpacks and we went on  the road and we learned to Train hop and  we lived on organic farms and then met  people and lived with anarchist  collectives and then lived in squats and  we went back and forth across the  country and we went back to LA and kind  of made two movies in that same sort of  spirit another earth and sound of my  voice which were very handmade sort of  collectivist films and we couldn't  really shake the experience of that  summer still so we started writing about  it I think as a way to make sense of how  it opened us and thought it would be  more fun to embed it is the story the  idea oh I had this idea let's flesh this  out a little bit well in a 6-day writing  week we spent a lot of time just doing  homework with each other just you know  housekeeping so how are you feeling how  am I feeling what are you reading you  know what am I reading sharing those  kind of things with each other and then  when we feel like the tracks are clear  and clean we start sending the ideas  back and forth and they start so I think  they start like amoebas they're so  gelatinous they're so fragile that you  know we never write on the computer or  anything for months we spend just  passing the gela